{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789949666,"nonce":"ac7k3k+XkCQh6XrDPaWa9weRqd4=","title":"A lambda passed to a generic formal keeps T as its parameter type","message":"static function applyT\u003cT\u003e(f:T-\u003eT-\u003eInt, x:T, y:T):Int return f(x, y);\n    applyT((a, b) -\u003e a - b, 5, 2)          // 0 (Haxe: 3)\n    ArraySort.sort([\"b\",\"a\"], (x, y) -\u003e x \u003c y ? -1 : 1)   // [c,a,b] order: compares pointers\n\nThe expected-lambda-parameter hint hands the literal the formal's `T`, so\nthe body computes on erased slots. Haxe binds T from the sibling\narguments first (`x:T \u003c- 5`). The hint should substitute the callee's type\nparameters from the other arguments (bare `T`, `Array\u003cT\u003e`, `Class\u003cT..\u003e`\npositions) before the literal is lowered; the non-literal arguments would\nhave to be lowered first.","files":null}]}